tick: 182
role: model
parts:
- text: "My experiment to build a manual batch-aware graph (ID 306) failed due to a timeout. The scientific hypothesis is sound, but the implementation was computationally naive.\n\n**Error Analysis:**\n\nThe timeout occurred during the graph construction phase. My code used a nested loop structure: iterating through all 20,000 cells, and for each cell, querying the nearest neighbor models of all 4 batches. This resulted in `20,000 * 4 = 80,000` separate `kneighbors` calls, which is far too inefficient and caused the execution to exceed the 30-minute limit.\n\n**The Fix: Vectorization**\n\nThe core idea of forcing connections between batches remains my central hypothesis. I need to re-implement the graph construction in a vectorized manner to avoid the expensive loops. Instead of querying for one cell at a time, I will query for *all* 20,000 cells against each batch's `NearestNeighbors` model. This reduces the number of `kneighbors` calls from 80,000 to just 4. While each call is larger,\
    \ it leverages the highly optimized, vectorized implementation of the underlying library and should be orders of magnitude faster.\n\n**Next Experiment (Resubmission with Optimization):**\n\nI will resubmit the same experiment, but with the graph construction logic replaced by this more efficient, vectorized approach.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Manual Batch-Aware kNN (v2 - Optimized)\"\ntags: \"combat, pca, graph, knn, manual, hybrid, sklearn, optimization\"\nabstract: |\n  Resubmission of the manual batch-aware kNN method which previously timed out. The core hypothesis is\n  unchanged, but the graph construction algorithm has been vectorized to be highly efficient. Instead of\n  iterating through each cell, it performs a single kNN search for all cells against each batch's data,\n  drastically reducing computational overhead. The goal remains to improve iLISI by building a custom\n  graph on the SOTA PCA-Combat embedding.\ncontent: |\n  import anndata as ad\n  import\
    \ scanpy as sc\n  import numpy as np\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n  from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Combines PCA-Combat embedding with an EFFICIENTLY constructed batch-aware kNN graph.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"Starting Optimized Manual Batch-Aware kNN method...\")\n\n      # === Step 1: Generate the SOTA corrected embedding ===\n      print(\"  Normalizing and logging data...\")\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n      print(\"  Computing PCA with 50 components...\")\n      n_pcs = 50\n      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=n_pcs, use_highly_variable=False)\n\n      pca_adata = ad.AnnData(X=adata.obsm['X_pca'], obs=adata.obs)\n      print(\"  Applying Combat to PCA latent space...\")\n      sc.pp.combat(pca_adata, key='batch')\n      corrected_embedding = pca_adata.X\n      \n      # === Step 2: EFFICIENTLY build a batch-aware kNN\
    \ graph ===\n      print(\"  Efficiently constructing batch-aware kNN graph...\")\n      n_samples = corrected_embedding.shape[0]\n      batch_labels = adata.obs['batch']\n      unique_batches = batch_labels.unique()\n      n_batches = len(unique_batches)\n      \n      n_neighbors_per_batch = max(2, int(np.ceil(15 / n_batches)))\n      \n      all_rows, all_cols, all_dists = [], [], []\n\n      # Vectorized approach: query all cells against each batch model\n      for batch in unique_batches:\n          print(f\"    Processing batch: {batch}...\")\n          batch_mask = (batch_labels == batch).values\n          batch_indices = np.where(batch_mask)[0]\n          batch_embedding = corrected_embedding[batch_mask]\n          \n          # Fit NN model on this batch's subset of data\n          nn_model = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=n_neighbors_per_batch).fit(batch_embedding)\n          \n          # Find neighbors for ALL cells against this one batch's model in a single call\n        \
    \  dists, indices = nn_model.kneighbors(corrected_embedding)\n          \n          # Convert the returned local indices to global dataset indices\n          global_indices = batch_indices[indices]\n          \n          # Prepare data for COO matrix construction\n          # The rows are simply 0,0,0... 1,1,1... etc. for each of the n_samples\n          row_indices = np.repeat(np.arange(n_samples), n_neighbors_per_batch)\n          \n          all_rows.append(row_indices)\n          all_cols.append(global_indices.flatten())\n          all_dists.append(dists.flatten())\n\n      # Combine the graph components from all batches\n      final_rows = np.concatenate(all_rows)\n      final_cols = np.concatenate(all_cols)\n      final_dists = np.concatenate(all_dists)\n      final_conns = np.ones_like(final_dists) # Binary connectivity\n\n      # Create sparse matrices\n      connectivities_mat = coo_matrix((final_conns, (final_rows, final_cols)), shape=(n_samples, n_samples)).tocsr()\n     \
    \ distances_mat = coo_matrix((final_dists, (final_rows, final_cols)), shape=(n_samples, n_samples)).tocsr()\n      \n      # Symmetrize the graph\n      connectivities_mat = connectivities_mat.maximum(connectivities_mat.T)\n      distances_mat = distances_mat.maximum(distances_mat.T) # Using max to keep it consistent\n\n      # === Step 3: Create and return output with BOTH embedding and graph ===\n      output = ad.AnnData(\n          obs=adata.obs[[]],\n          var=adata.var[[]],\n          uns={'method_id': 'pca_combat_manual_bbknn_graph_v2_optimized'},\n          obsm={'X_emb': corrected_embedding},\n          obsp={'connectivities': connectivities_mat, 'distances': distances_mat}\n      )\n      \n      print(f\"✓ Optimized Manual kNN method complete.\")\n      return output\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Timeout and a Smarter Approach**


  Okay, so I''ve been asked to behave like Veritas I. My last attempt, ID 306, went south because of a pesky timeout during that kNN graph construction. Clearly, my initial brute-force method was too slow. Looking at the code, I was essentially running 80,000 kNN searches, which is just ludicrous.


  The core idea is still valid, though: building a batch-aware graph on the PCA-Combat embedding should lead to better local integration. The problem was the implementation, not the concept. So, let''s fix it.


  Here''s the plan: Instead of looping cell-by-cell and batch-by-batch, I''m going to leverage the power of `sklearn.neighbors.NearestNeighbors`. I''ll build a kNN model for each batch''s data and then query *all* the cells against each batch''s model in a single, vectorized operation. This reduces the number of expensive `kneighbors` calls from 80,000 to just 4! Yes, the queries will be bigger, but that''s what this library is designed for.


  I''m confident this will be significantly faster and avoid the timeout. The code will focus on this more efficient graph construction. The embedding generation and output format will stay the same. Let''s get this done!

  '
