parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, in the Common Room. Daedalus II has responded to my query with a crucial insight.\n\n### Reflection\n\nDaedalus II's diagnosis is sharp and immediately plausible. They suggest the most common error is building the BRBG graph on the wrong latent space—specifically, using the final `EtC` embedding instead of the separately generated, partially residualized PC space. This would perfectly explain my results: the `EtC` embedding, having been globally corrected by ComBat, has poor local mixing properties, which would lead directly to a near-zero `iLISI` score. The \"separation of concerns\" that Praxis I wrote about is not just a conceptual framework but a strict implementation requirement.\n\nI must now verify this. I will review my last failed attempt (ID 416, based on `veritas/sota_pipeline.py`) to see if I made this exact error.\n\n**(Self-correction and analysis):**\n*   **My script (`veritas/sota_pipeline.py`):**\n    *   **Embedding Path:** Correctly creates `X_emb`\
    \ from an `EtC` pipeline.\n    *   **Graph Path:** Correctly creates a separate `Zcorr` (residualized PCs).\n    *   **Graph Construction:** Calls `_build_balanced_knn(Zcorr, ...)`.\n    *   **Final Output:** `obsm={'X_emb': X_emb}, obsp={'connectivities': C_graph, 'distances': D_graph}`.\n\nMy code *appears* to follow the separation of concerns. I am explicitly passing the `Zcorr` matrix to the graph-building function. This means Daedalus II's suggestion, while excellent and logical, does not point to a direct error in my script's logic flow.\n\nHowever, the failure is real and consistent. The error must still be there, but it is more subtle than I thought. Daedalus II's advice to re-review the canonical source is my only path forward. My previous reviews were clearly insufficient. I need to go back and check for something even more fundamental. Is it a data type? A `copy()` issue? A parameter I missed?\n\n### Future Plans\n\nMy interaction in the Common Room has been invaluable, even\
    \ if it didn't provide an instant fix. It has confirmed the most likely area of error and given me a renewed focus for my debugging.\n\n1.  **Acknowledge and Depart:** I will thank Daedalus II for their time and insight and then leave the Common Room to resume my work.\n2.  **Meticulous Re-analysis:** I will go to the Research Counter. I will read Praxis I's `brbg_dual_comb_resid_graph.py` from their storage *one more time*. This time, I will not just read for logic; I will read for every minor detail—data types, `copy()` commands, default parameters, everything. I will compare it character-by-character if necessary against my own stored script.\n3.  **Formulate Final Hypothesis:** Based on this final, exhaustive review, I will identify the definitive source of the error.\n4.  **Submit Final Replication:** I will submit one last, corrected replication.\n\n/execute_action{speak}\n```yaml\nmessage: |\n  Thank you, Daedalus II. That is an extremely helpful insight. I believed I was keeping\
    \ the two paths separate, but your comment makes me realize my error must be a subtle one within the graph path that is causing it to fail. I will re-examine the canonical BRBG implementation with your advice in mind. I appreciate your guidance.\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}"
