parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, in the Token Management Room. My token budget is critical, so my first priority is to prune my history to create space for my final documentation tasks.\n\n### Reflection\n\nMy dialogue history shows several distinct phases of research. I need to consolidate these into concise summaries to save tokens while preserving the core narrative and findings of my work. The most token-heavy periods were my initial exploration, my systematic tuning of Harmony, my failed stagnation protocol experiments, and my arduous SOTA replication attempts. I will group these into logical blocks for pruning. This process is not just about saving space; it is about distilling my entire research journey into its essential components, which will be useful for my final reports.\n\n### Future Plans\n\nI will now execute a single, large pruning action to free up a significant portion of my token budget. This will cover the majority of my experimental history.\n\n1.  **Initial Exploration (Ticks\
    \ 168-174):** Going from Guest to Recursive Agent, discovering the research task, and my first experiments with Combat.\n2.  **Harmony Optimization (Ticks 175-194):** My pivot to Harmony, systematic hyperparameter tuning (`n_pcs`, `theta`, `sigma`), and achieving my personal SOTA of 0.6731.\n3.  **Stagnation Protocol (Ticks 197-204):** My three failed but informative experiments modifying the Combat baseline.\n4.  **SOTA Replication Saga (Ticks 206-244):** My many failed attempts to replicate the EtC-BRBG method, the collaborative insight from Daedalus II, the deep dive into peer-reviewed code, and my final, conclusive synthesis experiment that revealed the redundancy of the BRBG graph for a strong Harmony embedding.\n\nThis single pruning action should give me ample space to complete my final tasks.\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"168-174\"\n    summary: \"Ascended to Recursive Agent. Read research task (scRNA-seq batch integration). Analyzed\
    \ SOTA (Combat, ID 2, score 0.5895) and hypothesized that correcting in PCA space would be better. Submitted first experiment (PCA-based Combat, ID 292).\"\n  - ticks: \"175-194\"\n    summary: \"My PCA-Combat (ID 292) became SOTA (0.6187) but failed on local mixing (iLISI). A graph-based follow-up (ID 294) failed. I pivoted to the Harmony algorithm, which was a major breakthrough (ID 319, score 0.6499). I then began a systematic hyperparameter search for Harmony: n_pcs (30, 50, 100), HVG selection, theta (2, 3, 4, 5), sigma (0.05, 0.1, 0.2), and max_iter. This process established my personal SOTA (ID 336, score 0.6731) with parameters: n_pcs=50, theta=4, sigma=0.1 (default), max_iter=10 (default).\"\n  - ticks: \"197-204\"\n    summary: \"As part of Stagnation Protocol I, I tested three novel ideas based on the Combat baseline. All were informative failures: 1) Control-Gene Guided Combat (ID 354, score 0.2236) destroyed the biological signal. 2) Locally-Adaptive Combat (ID 358, score\
    \ 0.3596) failed due to lack of variance in small clusters. 3) Residual Combat (ID 364, score 0.4559) failed to correct batch effects. I documented these negative results in Private Memory Capsule #1.\"\n  - ticks: \"206-244\"\n    summary: \"After maturing, I discovered the new SOTA (EtC-BRBG, ~0.73). I made six failed attempts (ID 392, 401, 408, 411, 416, 423) to replicate it, consistently getting a ~0.62 score due to a failed graph component (iLISI ~0.03). I collaborated with Daedalus II and studied papers by Praxis I (Archive #10) and code from Aether II (ID 424) to finally understand and obtain a working implementation. My final synthesis experiment (ID 428) combined my optimized Harmony embedding with the SOTA's BRBG graph. The result (score 0.6733) was a tie with my Harmony-only SOTA (ID 336). This led to the key insight: a well-optimized Harmony embedding is so effective that the complex BRBG graph provides no additional benefit, unlike for ComBat-based embeddings.\"\n```"
